#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107175 :tutorial/content "We have been using the following formula for the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a}= |\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| \\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nHowever, sometimes we are not given the angle between two vectors. In such cases, we can solve for $\\cos \\theta$ in terms of the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =|\\,a\\,|\\,|\\,b\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,|\\,|\\,b\\,|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our original formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =|\\,a\\,|\\cdot \\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,|\\,|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, another general formula for the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f213d39a-e24c-5b66-905d-b756335a134e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9667 :tutorial/title "Deriving a General Formula"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107176 :tutorial/content "Notice that if two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are perpendicular, then the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ (or $\\mathbf{b}$ onto $\\mathbf{a}$) is equal to $0.$\n\nFor example, consider the following illustration. Notice that, since $\\mathbf{a} \\perp \\mathbf{b},$ the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ doesn't extend in the direction of $\\mathbf{b}$ at all. So, the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ is $0.$\n\n![](images/ef/efb79d8645a5df54d76c0199335aad61b258e400a98dc485ccd8b3abc8e0ba8d.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following fact:\n\n*If $\\mathbf{a}$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{b}$ equals $0$ if and only if the vectors are perpendicular.*" :tutorial/id #uuid "6b0bca52-c737-5b73-a389-368042379fd3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9671 :tutorial/title "Projecting Perpendicular Vectors"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107177 :tutorial/content "The scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ can be negative. This happens when the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ extends in the direction opposite to that of $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n![](images/c1/c19648ea2df1935e89e10df2f7f5ab33ad537a5fdb52bb97c3555c9a0365701e.png)\n\nFor example, consider two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b},$ where it is known that \n\n$$\n|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,|=2, \\qquad \\theta = 135^\\circ.\n$$\n\nNotice that the angle $\\theta$ between the vectors is obtuse. \n\nComputing the scalar projection of $\\mathbf a$ onto $\\mathbf b$ using the formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({135}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, if $90^\\circ < \\theta \\leq 180^\\circ,$ then $\\cos\\theta <0$ and the scalar projection will be negative." :tutorial/id #uuid "1a12d95f-dd94-5328-b375-defd27770173" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9672 :tutorial/title "Negative Scalar Projections"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124443 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124247} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3991a54-342b-5cd0-87e2-606146240555" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient of a Function of Two Variables"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124444 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124248} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe2c7ce2-f053-57af-9f4d-54bd3a41648a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient of a Function of Three Variables"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124445 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124249} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8d807a6-7896-5045-a14a-908b58831340" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points Where a Gradient Vanishes"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124447 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124251} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67cfec73-130c-5b68-a2e0-5f12837bfc81" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient Using the Properties of the Gradient"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124452 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124256} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f2ac99b-55b2-5742-81ab-d2961db1250f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Functions at Points of Continuity"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124453 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124257} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5666035-199d-56aa-a6f4-f65a3f847b42" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Rational Functions by Factoring"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124454 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124258} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ae7e9c8-60e9-5f5e-afbf-1de6bb01bf07" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Line to a Level Curve"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124455 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124259} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "348fa49f-2af8-5562-91f6-3f70bb93ea90" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector to a Plane Curve"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124456 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124260} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84245283-3cf9-5889-8c42-896a0c8af291" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points on a Curve at which the Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to a Given Vector"}]}
